WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.090
You check their "last seen" one more time and tell yourself it's only because you care.

2
00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:10.340
But is that racing heart proof that you love them, or proof that you believe they belong to you?

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:14.950
Tonight we ask whether jealousy guards love or quietly strangles it.

4
00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.680
It's nearly one in the morning.

5
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.090
A phone lies face down on the table, and a short vibration makes it shudder.

6
00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:35.060
For a moment the screen glows, then it goes dark again.

7
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.590
The phone's owner is in the shower.

8
00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.240
The other person in the room looks at that light and feels something.

9
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.610
Nothing has happened yet.

10
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.740
There's no name, no message, no evidence.

11
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:52.190
There is only a vibration, and a question growing fast inside.

12
00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.400
Who is writing at this hour?

13
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.130
We know that feeling.

14
00:00:56.130 --> 00:01:00.260
A knot in the stomach, heat rising at the back of the neck.

15
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:11.110
Within a few seconds the mind builds a whole story: last week's meeting that ran late, the way the phone always seems to be face down lately, the answer that came a little too quickly when asked.

16
00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:14.120
The pieces fall into place on their own.

17
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.570
And strangely, the darker the picture, the more convincing it looks.

18
00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:25.740
In Shakespeare's Othello, Iago calls this feeling the green-eyed monster, and says it mocks the very meat it feeds on.

19
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.230
The monster's favorite food is not certainty.

20
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:31.120
It is doubt.

21
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.810
So where does this feeling come from?

22
00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:40.340
The first answer that comes to mind is this: I love, and so I am jealous.

23
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.310
If I didn't care, the light on that phone wouldn't matter to me.

24
00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.680
There is some truth in that answer.

25
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.490
No one is jealous of someone who has no place in their life.

26
00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.300
Jealousy shows that a bond exists.

27
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.470
But it does not show what kind of bond it is.

28
00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:06.560
And that is where the question of this episode begins: is jealousy proof of love, or the sign of something else that has crept into love, the urge to possess?

29
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.450
First, we need to separate two ideas, because in everyday speech they are always getting mixed up.

30
00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:18.500
Envy is wanting something that someone else has: the neighbor's car, a colleague's promotion.

31
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.790
Jealousy is the fear of losing something you believe is already yours.

32
00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:27.880
Envy involves two people: me, and the other who holds what I want.

33
00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:33.770
Jealousy involves three: me, the one I love, and whoever might come between us.

34
00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:38.860
At the root of jealousy lies not desire, but fear.

35
00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.390
What kind of fear?

36
00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:45.040
On the surface, the fear of being abandoned.

37
00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.370
Scratch a little deeper and other layers appear.

38
00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:52.020
The fear that my place could be filled by someone else.

39
00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.670
The fear that I am not good enough, not interesting enough.

40
00:02:55.670 --> 00:03:02.520
And perhaps, deepest of all, the fear that without someone's love I would not know who I am.

41
00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:13.370
The French thinker Roland Barthes, in the book where he took apart the language of love fragment by fragment, writes that the jealous person suffers four times over: they suffer because they are jealous,

42
00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:23.100
they suffer because they blame themselves for being jealous, they suffer because they fear their jealousy will hurt the one they love, and they suffer because they have given in to so ordinary a feeling.

43
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.910
Not one of these four has anything to do with the other person.

44
00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:30.720
All of them turn within the jealous person's own inner world.

45
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.490
The person looking at the phone isn't really curious about the name on the screen.

46
00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.820
They want to know whether their own place is secure.

47
00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:48.870
The difference looks small, but nearly every philosopher who has thought about jealousy has pointed to exactly this: at the center of jealousy may stand not the beloved, but the lover.

48
00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:54.440
In seventeenth-century Europe, a great wave of thinking about the passions swept through.

49
00:03:54.440 --> 00:04:03.210
The philosophers of the age of reason began to see the human being not only as a thinking creature, but as one whose emotions could also be studied in an orderly way.

50
00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.900
Jealousy did not escape this study.

51
00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:16.750
In sixteen forty-nine, one year before his death, René Descartes published Les Passions de l'âme, The Passions of the Soul, a work in which he defined the passions one by one.

52
00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:23.280
Descartes describes jealousy as a kind of fear: a fear tied to the desire to hold on to some good.

53
00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.730
But what is truly interesting is that he connects this fear to proportion.

54
00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:34.740
For him, whether jealousy is honorable depends on the value of what is being guarded, and on whether the fear is justified.

55
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:41.430
A commander defending a vital position deserves praise for staying alert to every possible attack.

56
00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:46.360
But for a man who is jealous of his wife, Descartes speaks far more harshly.

57
00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:54.810
Such jealousy, in his view, shows that the man does not love her rightly, and that he holds a poor opinion either of himself or of her.

58
00:04:54.810 --> 00:05:01.340
In other words, jealousy is a confession that the jealous person does not trust either themselves or the one they love.

59
00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:07.230
Turn back to the scene with the phone, and Descartes' observation is unsettlingly accurate.

60
00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:19.040
Two sentences run through the mind of the person staring at that light on the screen: "I am not enough," and "They are not faithful." Both are born less of love than of insecurity.

61
00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:28.290
About thirty years after Descartes, in sixteen seventy-seven, Baruch Spinoza's Ethica, known in English as the Ethics, was published after his death.

62
00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:34.980
Spinoza sets out the passions in propositions, like a geometry textbook.

63
00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:46.470
His definition of jealousy is just as cool and composed: if a person imagines that the thing they love is joined to someone else by a bond as close as, or closer than, the one that ties them to it,

64
00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:51.080
they will feel hatred toward the thing they love and envy toward that other person.

65
00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:59.370
Spinoza calls this love mixed with hatred jealousy, and he defines it as a wavering of the mind, born of love and hatred at the same time.

66
00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:02.860
There are two important details inside this definition.

67
00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:13.070
The first is the phrase "if a person imagines." For Spinoza, jealousy does not arise from an event itself, but from the picture of that event in the mind.

68
00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:17.360
What matters is not what actually happened, but what we believe happened.

69
00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.650
The second is that love and hatred can exist at the same moment.

70
00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:28.820
The jealous person is furious with the one they love and wants to punish them, yet at the same time is terrified of losing them.

71
00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:35.830
This swinging back and forth wears a person down, because it means being pulled in two opposite directions at once.

72
00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:41.560
In Spinoza's system, freedom means replacing the passive passions with understanding.

73
00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:46.330
To the extent that we understand an emotion, we stop being its prisoner.

74
00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:53.180
To understand jealousy, to see that it is a product of the imagination, is to loosen the first link in the chain.

75
00:06:53.180 --> 00:07:04.830
In France, in the same century, another figure, less a philosopher than an observer of court life, François de La Rochefoucauld, fit this idea into a single sentence in his book of Maxims,

76
00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:10.400
first printed in sixteen sixty-five: in jealousy there is more self-love than love.

77
00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:17.570
La Rochefoucauld believed that behind most human behavior lay selfishness, vanity, and the hunger for praise.

78
00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:24.580
He read jealousy in that light as well: what is wounded in the jealous person is not their love, but their pride.

79
00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:31.590
The Maxims contain one more sentence on jealousy, and perhaps an even sharper one: jealousy feeds on doubt.

80
00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:36.680
The moment doubt turns into certainty, it either becomes rage or comes to an end.

81
00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:40.650
This observation explains why jealousy can last so long.

82
00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:46.380
Faced with a certain betrayal, a person either breaks and walks away, or forgives.

83
00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.630
But as long as the uncertainty remains, the mind never stops working.

84
00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:58.160
Every glow of a phone screen, every late arrival, every distracted glance becomes fresh fuel.

85
00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:01.170
What these three thinkers share is clear.

86
00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:04.980
None of them sees jealousy as a pure measure of love.

87
00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:15.670
Descartes sees in it a lack of trust in oneself and in the other; Spinoza, a swing between love and hatred built by the imagination; and La Rochefoucauld, self-love in disguise.

88
00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:18.680
But this picture may be one-sided.

89
00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:26.810
Let us recall Descartes's commander: if we consider certain things worth protecting, could being watchful on their behalf also be a virtue?

90
00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:32.220
The Islamic intellectual tradition approaches this question with a very different concept.

91
00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:40.350
In Turkish, alongside the word for jealousy, kıskançlık, stands an older and weightier word: gayret.

92
00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:51.520
Today we mostly use this word to mean effort, as in "make an effort." But its Arabic root describes the protective sensitivity a person feels when someone else reaches for what they hold dear,

93
00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:55.330
especially the honor and privacy of their family.

94
00:08:55.330 --> 00:09:00.740
In old Turkish texts, gayret and kıskançlık often appear side by side.

95
00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:05.990
The Islamic ethical tradition neither glorified this feeling nor rejected it outright.

96
00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:09.160
Instead, it tried to draw a boundary around it.

97
00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:18.090
In a narration found in the authentic hadith collections, the jealousy of Sa'd ibn Ubadah, one of the Companions, is described to the Prophet.

98
00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.940
The Prophet does not condemn it.

99
00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:26.190
He says that he himself has more gayret than Sa'd, and that God's gayret is greater still.

100
00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:31.600
In this narration, gayret appears as the very opposite of indifference toward what is precious.

101
00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:39.570
In this tradition, a person who finds nothing worth protecting, who does not care when the honor of a loved one is violated, is not praised.

102
00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:44.820
Yet within the same tradition, another narration divides this feeling in two.

103
00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:53.110
According to a hadith recorded in the collections of Abu Dawud and al-Nasa'i, there are kinds of gayret that God loves and kinds He does not.

104
00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:58.360
The beloved kind is the gayret felt when there is a real cause for suspicion.

105
00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:03.130
The unloved kind is gayret felt when there is no cause for suspicion at all.

106
00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:07.100
This distinction speaks directly to the question of this episode.

107
00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:12.190
Gayret is a response, and it is legitimate only when it has a real cause.

108
00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:16.160
Jealousy without cause is not a virtue, but a flaw.

109
00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:20.610
We can capture this distinction with another old Turkish word: vehim.

110
00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:26.180
Vehim means perceiving something that is not there as though it were, a groundless fancy.

111
00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:31.750
If gayret is a well-founded concern, vehim is a suspicion that feeds on itself.

112
00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:36.840
Spinoza's emphasis on the imagination echoes here in another language.

113
00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:44.810
Both are saying the same thing: the most dangerous form of jealousy is the one that rests not on reality, but on the mind's own fiction.

114
00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:52.140
Surah al-Hujurat in the Quran contains a verse that shows, step by step, how this chain of events unfolds.

115
00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:58.030
The verse first tells us to avoid much suspicion, because some suspicion is a sin.

116
00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:02.800
Right after that, it says: do not spy on one another's private affairs.

117
00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:05.330
The order is striking.

118
00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:11.060
First a suspicion is born, and then that suspicion drives a person to go looking for proof.

119
00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:18.070
The one who glances at a phone screen in the night soon begins to wonder about that phone's password.

120
00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:23.800
The verse counsels us to stop at the very first link in the chain, at the suspicion itself.

121
00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:33.530
Imam al-Ghazali, in his great work written at the end of the eleventh century and the beginning of the twelfth, the Ihya Ulum al-Din, or The Revival of the Religious Sciences,

122
00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:37.980
takes up this subject on its own when he discusses the etiquette of marriage.

123
00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:42.110
Al-Ghazali's approach is a typical search for the middle way.

124
00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:47.840
On one side lies a slackness that is indifferent to a spouse's honor and cares about nothing at all.

125
00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:54.210
On the other lies a suspiciousness that watches the spouse constantly and looks for ill intent in every move.

126
00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:57.060
Al-Ghazali counts both as faults.

127
00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:07.910
He advises against prying into a spouse's private affairs and against thinking ill of them without cause, and he quotes a saying attributed to Ali: Do not be excessively jealous over your wife,

128
00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:11.720
or else evil will be imputed to her because of you.

129
00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:15.050
This saying reveals a hidden cost of jealousy.

130
00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:21.900
Excessive jealousy does not only wear down the jealous person; it also stains the one who is the object of that jealousy.

131
00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:28.590
Someone who is constantly suspected becomes suspect in the eyes of those around them, even if they have done nothing at all.

132
00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:34.320
Without realizing it, the jealous person damages the reputation of the very one they love.

133
00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:39.250
The broader framework of Islamic moral philosophy fits this picture as well.

134
00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:51.700
In the tradition that runs from Ibn Miskawayh to Nasir al-Din al-Tusi, and from there to Kınalızade Ali Efendi, who wrote the Akhlaq-i Alai in the sixteenth century, virtue is the balance between two extremes.

135
00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:59.510
Courage stands midway between cowardice and recklessness; generosity, midway between miserliness and extravagance.

136
00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:03.480
Protective zeal, too, can be weighed on these scales.

137
00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:08.570
Its deficiency is indifference, not caring for the one you love or for your own honor.

138
00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:12.860
Its excess is delusion, becoming a captive of suspicion.

139
00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:20.990
The virtue lies between the two: being watchful when there is reason for doubt, and being able to trust in peace when there is none.

140
00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:25.760
There is an interesting similarity between Descartes's commander and this framework.

141
00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:32.450
Descartes, too, had considered it honorable to stay on guard when what is being protected is valuable and the threat is real.

142
00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:35.780
But there is also a clear difference between the two.

143
00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:45.030
While the Islamic tradition ties protective zeal largely to the concepts of honor and privacy, it thinks of jealousy not as a right of ownership, but as a responsibility.

144
00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:54.120
This difference brings us to the final question of this episode: could the way we define our relationship with the person we love also shape the form our jealousy takes?

145
00:13:54.120 --> 00:14:00.010
The accounts of Aisha's jealousy show how realistically this tradition looks at human emotion.

146
00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:08.620
In one account, Aisha names the Prophet's first wife, Khadija, as the person she was most jealous of, even though she had never seen her.

147
00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:15.150
The reason was that the Prophet still spoke of Khadija with love years later, and sent gifts to her friends.

148
00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:17.680
There was no living rival.

149
00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:20.370
The jealousy was aimed at a memory.

150
00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:26.580
It is impossible not to notice that this jealousy rested on no evidence; it was born of a fear and a longing.

151
00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:33.750
Another account tells how Aisha, in a moment of anger, broke a dish of food sent by another of the Prophet's wives.

152
00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:36.760
The Prophet scolded no one.

153
00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:43.130
He said, "Your mother was overcome by jealousy," and sent an unbroken dish to replace the broken one.

154
00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:47.420
Jealousy is accepted as part of the human condition.

155
00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:52.030
But one does not surrender to it; it is met with calm compassion.

156
00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:54.560
Does the one who loves feel jealousy?

157
00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:58.530
An honest answer to this question has to be yes.

158
00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:03.300
Love is a bond, and every bond brings with it the possibility of breaking.

159
00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:08.550
Someone who claims never to feel jealous is either a saint or someone afraid of attachment.

160
00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:13.480
The real question is not whether jealousy exists, but what it turns into.

161
00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:17.130
What decides this is how we see the person we love.

162
00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:21.900
If we see them as a possession, jealousy turns into a claim of ownership.

163
00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:30.190
The owner of a possession considers himself entitled to know where it is, to control who touches it, and, if need be, to lock it away.

164
00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:39.280
Going through a phone, demanding passwords, interrogating someone about who they have been talking to: these are the natural extensions of this way of seeing.

165
00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:44.370
And every act of control makes the relationship look a little more like a prison.

166
00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:54.740
The German-born thinker Erich Fromm, in his book To Have or To Be, published in nineteen seventy-six, describes how modern people have turned even love into a mode of having.

167
00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:58.550
In the having mode, love becomes an object.

168
00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:03.160
It is acquired, kept, guarded, and its loss is feared.

169
00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:09.850
In the being mode, by contrast, love is an activity, something done anew again and again.

170
00:16:09.850 --> 00:16:13.980
According to Fromm, one human being cannot possess another.

171
00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:23.550
The moment we try to possess, we have already begun to kill the thing we love, because what makes it alive and free is precisely that it cannot be possessed.

172
00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:30.560
Islamic thought arrives at the same intuition through a different word: amanah, a trust held on behalf of another.

173
00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:39.970
In this understanding, nothing a person holds, not their wealth, not their body, not even the people they love, truly belongs to them in any final sense.

174
00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:43.300
All of it has been placed in their care for a time.

175
00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:52.070
A verse in Surah al-Ahzab tells of the trust that the heavens, the earth and the mountains shrank from bearing, and that humankind took upon itself.

176
00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:55.880
A trust is both a burden and an honor at once.

177
00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:57.770
But it is not ownership.

178
00:16:57.770 --> 00:17:03.820
Seeing the person we love as a trust does not make jealousy itself disappear.

179
00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:09.070
A trust, too, is guarded; one worries over a trust, too.

180
00:17:09.070 --> 00:17:12.400
The difference lies in the direction of that guarding.

181
00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:15.410
The owner of a property protects his own rights.

182
00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:21.140
The trustee, on the other hand, watches over the well-being of what has been left in his care.

183
00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:35.190
The first says, "they are mine." The second says, "I must take good care of them." In the first view, the other person is an object; in the second, they are a human being with a will of their own, a path of their own,

184
00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:37.720
secrets of their own.

185
00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:45.530
A trustee's jealousy, as al-Ghazali described it, does not spy on the other person, yet neither does it remain indifferent to them.

186
00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:50.460
This way of seeing has a hard side as well, and there is no point in hiding it.

187
00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:54.270
A trust can one day be taken back.

188
00:17:54.270 --> 00:18:00.000
The person we love may leave, may choose another path, life itself may take them from us.

189
00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:08.130
The gaze of ownership resists this possibility; it tightens its control, trying to eliminate the chance of loss before it can ever arrive.

190
00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:12.900
The gaze of trust, by contrast, accepts loss as part of life.

191
00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:15.590
This leaves a person more vulnerable.

192
00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:17.960
But also more free.

193
00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:27.530
Because only where control is released does real trust become possible for the first time, and trust is the name not only of faith in the other, but of a person's own inner peace.

194
00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:30.860
Let us return to the phone on the table at night.

195
00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:33.870
The screen lights up again.

196
00:18:33.870 --> 00:18:38.160
This time, the person looking at that glow pauses for a moment.

197
00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:43.570
They feel the knot inside them, but they do not immediately turn it into a story.

198
00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:48.180
They ask themselves: Does this fear come from them, or from me?

199
00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:51.990
Is there a real reason here, or only my own imaginings?

200
00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:54.840
Perhaps the answer is not easy.

201
00:18:54.840 --> 00:19:02.010
Perhaps there truly is something that needs to be talked about, and then speaking openly and honestly is the way to honor that devotion.

202
00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:04.380
But perhaps there is not.

203
00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:12.350
In that case, the hardest thing to do, and perhaps the greatest act of love, is to leave the phone right where it lies, face down.

204
00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:16.480
Is jealousy the proof of love, or the chain of possession?

205
00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:18.690
Perhaps it is neither.

206
00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:22.340
Perhaps jealousy is simply a test.

207
00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:29.830
A mirror that shows whether a person carries their love as property or as a trust, whether they lean on their fear or on their trust.

208
00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:34.760
What we see in the mirror depends, in part, on how we choose to look into it.

209
00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:38.730
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

210
00:19:38.730 --> 00:19:41.900
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
